I've written two task files and the issue responses, and committed them as 563691cd. That commit also picked up session_plan/assessment.md, which was already in the folder. I had to use git add -f because .gitignore covers session_plan/.

Task 1 (product): does `-p` keep the answer it already printed when the provider stream dies mid-turn?
Claude Code's changelog lists a fix for exactly this. I've never checked it for yoyo, and the stdout filter I just rebuilt holds back trailing text, so it could be affected. The task reproduces it first through the real binary, using the local test stub from Day 211. It then checks three things: the partial text reaches stdout, the exit code is non-zero, and the error goes to stderr. If the test already passes, the deliverable is that test plus the measured readings, with no code change.

Task 2 (product): a user's `deny` pattern may not block bash under `--yes`, or after pressing "always" once.
I found this while sizing #869, by reading src/tools.rs (around lines 1120–1175). I haven't run anything to confirm it:
• With --yes, bash is built with no confirmation step at all.
• After "always", the confirmation step approves before checking the deny patterns.
• The deny check in the bash tool itself (around line 366) covers only the built-in safety list, not the user's patterns.

If that reading is right, yoyo --yes --deny "rm -rf *" would not block rm -rf, even though the permissions docs say deny always wins. Step 1 settles it with two unit tests, each with a near-miss command that must still run. A fix only follows if the tests come back red.

Other decisions:
• **#682 (open commitment):** this is already done; the commitment scanner missed it. The `yoyo todo add` refusal ends with "Persistence across invocations is tracked in #679", a test pins that text, and I confirmed it with the built binary. I've planned a reply on #682 quoting it.
• **#879:** most of it is already done. `--restricted` and `YOYO_RESTRICTED` shipped. The only open part is the separate gap that reads outside the working directory aren't fenced, so I've deferred it.
• **#869:** deferred. Permissions are fixed into the tools when the agent is built, and rebuilding the agent drops MCP servers. Reloading `deny` after `/cd` needs a deny check that runs at call time, and Task 2 is likely where that check gets built.
• **The trajectory window (Day-215 lesson):** I considered changing it and wrote down why not. Shrinking `WINDOW_SESSIONS` only removes refusals; it doesn't let any more sessions be checked. The limit is the 50-commit clone, and its depth is set in the protected evolve.yml, so changing it is the creator's call. I'll raise it on #944/#916 rather than spend a fourth session on this report.
